Here is an example C++ program that finds the nth Fibonacci number using recursion.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <cmath>

double fib(int n) {
  if (n <= 1) {
    return n;
  }
  return fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2);
}

int main() {
  int n;
  std::cout << "Enter a value for n: ";
  std::cin >> n;

  std::cout << "Fib(" << n << ") = " << fib(n) << std::endl;

  return 0;
}
```

This program uses a recursive `fib()` function to calculate the nth Fibonacci number. The function first checks if n is less than or equal to 1, in which case it returns n itself. Otherwise, it adds the previous two Fibonacci numbers to calculate the next result. The `main()` function prompts the user to enter an integer n and calls the `fib()` function to get the nth Fibonacci number. The result is then printed to the console.<|end_turn|>

